+/*
+ * P4' (eighth stage): the entries of the table that can put pixels down and that the wire does not
+ * carry. spec.def holds the 62 calls CoreAnimation is seen to make, and a frame's record is nine
+ * triangles through glDrawArrays/glDrawElements; the glyphs -- the text, the Dock, the search field
+ * -- are in none of them, so with the host drawing the frame (remote_only) the picture comes back
+ * without them. If they are drawn here at all it is through one of the entries below, none of which
+ * has a recorder in tools/glwire/spec.def and so none has a row in the record stream.
+ *
+ * Boot-arg glslots=1: the first call of one of them says so, and every two hundredth frame says how
+ * many each has had (the counts eagl_enter() keeps for the whole table). Without it nothing here
+ * runs. With it the answer to "which entry draws the glyph" is a log line -- or the absence of one,
+ * which is "not an entry of the EAGL table at all" (see watched_slots_report()).
+ *
+ * The names are the table's own (src/eagl_slots.txt), Darwin's spelling of the entry: the two
+ * instanced draws are also known as glDrawArraysInstanced and glDrawElementsInstanced, and
+ * glVertexAttribDivisor is not a draw -- it is what an instanced draw needs set up first, so a call
+ * to it is half the evidence. glMultiDrawArrays, glMultiDrawElements, glDrawArraysIndirect and
+ * glDrawElementsIndirect are not entries of Darwin's table at all (no offset in src/eagl_slots.txt),
+ * so no call through OpenGLES.framework can reach them and they cannot be the answer.
